"""Narrative of the loop-17 report (version 2, after the two reviews). sections(g) gets the globals of build_loop17_report.py and returns the html blocks.
Every number that can change is read from results_master.json (MJ), results_extra.json (EX) and the route JSONs through the accessors; each experiment has a Hypothesis / Result / Conclusion block."""
import html

esc = html.escape


def sections(g):
    S, bs, f3, figure, table = g["S"], g["bs"], g["f3"], g["figure"], g["table"]
    MJ, EX, PERT3, PERT4, NAME, rb = g["MJ"], g["EX"], g["PERT3"], g["PERT4"], g["NAME"], g["rb"]
    has = lambda p, k: k in MJ["perts"][p]["cards"]
    P3 = PERT3

    def rs(tag, ph="fail", sig="0"):
        c = MJ["route"].get(tag, {})
        xs = [v["route_success"] for k, v in c.items() if k.startswith(f"sigma{sig}_") and k.endswith(f"_{ph}")]
        return sum(xs) / len(xs) if xs else float("nan")

    def seeds(tag, ph="fail", sig="0"):
        c = MJ["route"].get(tag, {})
        xs = sorted(v["route_success"] for k, v in c.items() if k.startswith(f"sigma{sig}_") and k.endswith(f"_{ph}"))
        return " / ".join(f"{x:.2f}" for x in xs)

    def share(p, k, base="champ_single", top="champ_single"):
        """share of the S loss (clean half minus failure half of the base) that system k wins back"""
        loss = S(p, top, "cl") - S(p, base)
        return (S(p, k) - S(p, base)) / loss

    def trio(fn, nd=3):
        return " / ".join(f3(fn(p), nd) for p in P3)

    def b(p, key):
        return bs(p, key)

    h = []
    cl = S("dark", "champ_single", "cl")
    ref = EX.get("cleanref", {})
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ title and summary
    h.append("<h1>Loop 17: the lane-graph chain under sensor failure</h1>")
    h.append("<div class='sub'>Robustness, temporal fusion and map assignment of the fully differentiable chain (camera + radar foundation, sparse lane-graph builder, route head, planner consumer) &middot; validation scenes only, test frames not touched &middot; 2026-10-24 / 25</div>")
    h.append("<h2>Summary</h2>")
    mo = EX.get("maponly") or {}
    mo0, mo1, mo2 = (mo.get(k, {}) for k in ("sigma0", "sigma1", "sigma2"))
    h.append(f"""<div class='box'><p><b>Question.</b> The loop-16 champion is the end-to-end candidate (a sparse lane graph, 14 vertices per 100 m, with a navigation attribute for a planner). It had never been tested where a vehicle needs it: when a sensor fails. Here it is tested on 22 validation scenes whose second half is perturbed (night, motion blur, three front cameras off; four milder types in section 4.2). Order of every triple below: night / blur / three front cameras off.</p>
<p><b>Answer in two lines.</b> Without a map, the best chain is the champion with a learned gated memory on the input ({trio(lambda p: S(p, 'gate'))} against {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'norec_single'))} without memory, {f3(cl)} on clean frames); training the builder on perturbed frames is the larger lever for single frames. The SD map adds little at realistic registration errors; the large numbers with an exact map are an upper bound, because the map in these experiments is derived from the lane map.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>What a failure costs.</b> The failure half scores S {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'champ_single'))} (clean half {f3(cl)}; the same frames without perturbation: {f3(ref.get('dark', {}).get('champ_single', {}).get('S', float('nan')))}, section 4.1). The control chain E94 loses as much ({trio(lambda p: S(p, 'ctrl_single'))}). What is found is also less precise (P@1 0.91 to {f3(S('dark','champ_single',m='P@1.0'),2)} at night, false length x4); most of the loss is lanes not found ({f3(S('dark','champ_single',m='lanes_found'),2)} against {f3(S('dark','champ_single','cl',m='lanes_found'),2)}).</li>
<li><b>A memory on the input.</b> The training-free spatial hold wins back {share('dark','champ_hold')*100:.0f} % / {share('blur','champ_hold')*100:.0f} % / {share('cam_front3_off','champ_hold')*100:.0f} % of the S loss (F1@1 {b('dark','champ_hold - champ_single')} at night). On the clean half S is unchanged, J falls from 0.247 to 0.222. With the same hold the champion has lower F1@1 than the control at night ({b('dark','champ_hold - ctrl_hold')}), equal in blur, higher with three cameras off; its higher S rests partly on sparsity, and it finds fewer lanes than the control (0.44 against 0.56 at night).</li>
<li><b>A learned gated memory</b> (12.5 k parameters, trained out-of-fold on 11 scenes) adds {S('dark','gate')-S('dark','norec_hold'):+.3f} / {S('blur','gate')-S('blur','norec_hold'):+.3f} / {S('cam_front3_off','gate')-S('cam_front3_off','norec_hold'):+.3f} S over the hold (F1@1 {b('dark','gate - norec_hold')} at night, {b('blur','gate - norec_hold')} in blur, {b('cam_front3_off','gate - norec_hold')} with lost cameras): a small gain at night and in blur, none with lost cameras, and it needs failure data to train.</li>
<li><b>Training on perturbed frames.</b> The builder trained on 842 perturbed training rows per type (no map, control C) finds {f3(S('dark','m139c_single_mnone',m='lanes_found'),2)} of the lanes at night on single frames instead of {f3(S('dark','norec_single',m='lanes_found'),2)} (F1@1 {b('dark','m139c_single_mnone - norec_single')}). The earlier null result (fine-tuning on about 80 frames per type, section 4.5) was a sample-size result, not a law. With the hold, C is no better than the champion.</li>
<li><b>The SD map.</b> The map alone, drawn as a lane graph, scores S {f3(mo0.get('S'))} with exact registration, {f3(mo1.get('S'))} with a 1 m error and {f3(mo2.get('S'))} with 2 m (section 4.7): an exact map is close to the answer. The map input of the trained builder adds {S('dark','m139b_hold_m0')-S('dark','m139b_hold_mnone'):+.3f} / {S('blur','m139b_hold_m0')-S('blur','m139b_hold_mnone'):+.3f} / {S('cam_front3_off','m139b_hold_m0')-S('cam_front3_off','m139b_hold_mnone'):+.3f} S with the hold (exact map against no map), {S('dark','gateB_m1')-S('dark','gateB_mnone'):+.3f} with a 1 m error behind the gate, {S('dark','gateB_m2')-S('dark','gateB_mnone'):+.3f} with 2 m; a wrong map costs F1@1 {b('dark','gateB_mwrong - gateB_mnone')} at night. Route success gains more (table 4.7).</li>
<li><b>Route success is the fragile attribute.</b> {rs('e135_route/dark_single'):.2f} / {rs('e135_route/blur_single'):.2f} / {rs('e135_route/cam_front3_off_single'):.2f} on single frames against {rs('e135_route/dark_single','cl'):.2f} clean; the hold and the gate bring it to about {rs('e135_route/dark_gate'):.2f} / {rs('e135_route/blur_gate'):.2f} / {rs('e135_route/cam_front3_off_gate'):.2f}. The two route-head seeds differ by 0.05 to 0.09, so route differences below about 0.1 are not interpreted without the intervals of section 4.6.</li>
<li><b>Joint end-to-end training</b> (the route loss through the node tokens into the builder) works mechanically; the experiment is too small to show a gain (section 4.8): no planner loss, one seed, a route loss already near zero.</li>
<li><b>Time</b> {g['latency_total']}; the foundation network is not included.</li>
</ul></div>""")
    h.append("<h3>Terms used in the tables</h3>")
    h.append(table(["term", "meaning"],
                   [["S", "composite score of the lane-graph experiments of loop 16: 0.30 F1@1 + 0.20 F1@0.5 + 0.20 J + 0.15 sparsity + 0.15 (1 - false length); it rewards sparse graphs"],
                    ["F1@1, F1@0.5, P@1, R@1", "point F1 / precision / recall of the graph against the ground-truth lane graph at 1 m (0.5 m) tolerance"],
                    ["J", "mean of the split and merge F1 at 6 m: how well junctions are found"],
                    ["lanes found", "share of the ground-truth lane length matched within the tolerance"],
                    ["v / 100 m, false length", "vertices per 100 m of graph (sparsity; the Douglas-Peucker floor is about 14); share of the predicted length that is not on a lane"],
                    ["failure series, clean half, failure half", "22 validation scenes, 291 frames per failure type (every third key frame, 1.5 s apart); the first half of each scene is unperturbed, the second half (about 10 s) uses perturbed foundation features"],
                    ["single frame, hold, gate", "no memory; spatial hold (per pixel the decayed, ego-warped previous input replaces the current one where its lane heat is higher); learned gate between the warped previous input and the current frame"],
                    ["time recursion", "the builder's own memory: nodes of the previous frame's graph as extra seeds; 'recursion off' is used with the hold and the gate"],
                    ["out-of-fold", "the 22 scenes are split in two folds of 11; a learned part is trained on one fold and evaluated on the other, both ways"],
                    ["arm B / arm C", "the map-conditioned builder trained on perturbed training rows with the SD map (B) and the same training with the map always zero (C)"],
                    ["route success", "per frame: the edges flagged as route cover and are covered by the ground-truth route points (1.5 m) to at least 80 %; frames without a ground-truth route or without a graph are dropped (the counts are given)"],
                    ["u", "training unit, 1200 iterations of 8 frames"]]))

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ chain
    h.append("<h2>1. The chain and what is differentiable</h2>")
    h.append(figure("p17_arch.png", "The robust chain. Blue: learned, green: parameter-free but differentiable, grey dashed: export at inference (the assembly of the beam into a graph, a selection; the gradient flows through the kept nodes and their tokens), violet dashed: the gradient path of the route loss (E202). No planner loss was used in this loop."))
    h.append("<p>A frame passes the frozen camera + radar foundation (GaussianCaR, 39 BEV channels), the dense head (lane heat and direction, 3 channels) and, new in this loop, a memory stage: the previous fused input, warped by the ego motion, is mixed with the current frame by a gate. The 42 channels feed the builder D7 (1.2 M parameters). Its assembled graph carries node tokens; the route head reads them every 4 m along the edges and cross-attends to the SD-map raster; the consumer predicts the path at 20 m. In the map-conditioned variant (section 4.7) the raster is also an input of the builder. Time per frame: section 4.9.</p>")

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ literature (short)
    h.append("<h2>2. Building blocks and literature</h2>")
    h.append("<p>Three literature agents wrote 103 cards (<code>report_final/text/lit17/</code>, from page digests, not equations; they mark links they did not open). What they say about the blocks tried here, and what was not tried:</p>")
    h.append(table(["block", "tried here", "what the cards say (not verified by us)"],
                   [["warp + hold / EMA memory of the BEV input", "yes (E195, E196)", "temporal fusion is the cheap lever under camera loss (MapBench 2406.12214: +3 to +6 mAP for StreamMapNet-style memory); memory beyond about 4 frames saturates"],
                    ["gated memory write", "yes (E199, E207)", "SIGMA-Lane 2608.16338 gates the write into a Mamba state against contaminated frames; the Post Fusion Stabilizer 2603.05623 is a 3.3 M adapter between a frozen camera-LiDAR fusion and a 3D detection head (+4.4 mAP at low light, +5.7 ms), without a temporal element"],
                    ["memory of the module's own prediction", "yes, recursion (E196): no effect", "StreamMapNet 2308.12570, MapTracker 2403.15951; no paper tests it for a lane graph with forks under modality failure"],
                    ["training on perturbed data / clean teacher", "yes (E198, E204, E206)", "SafeMap 2507.00861 (clean-teacher loss on BEV features); not tried: the teacher loss"],
                    ["cross-attention to an SD map", "yes (route head; E204, E206)", "NavMapFusion 2512.03317 (1 m shift noise in training holds to 3 m; 10 % wrong maps limit the drop), UMPE 2605.02762 (missing map as a trained mode), ONR / MAT 2507.07487 (closest to our route head)"],
                    ["matching with an abstain output (dustbin)", "no", "SuperGlue 1911.11763, LightGlue 2306.13643; no driving paper tests a planner against a wrong route"],
                    ["joint training through the node tokens", "yes (E202), inconclusive", "route-as-lane-mask planners (GC-PGP 2302.07753, Diffusion Planner 2501.15564); shortcut risk: Hidden Biases 2306.07957"]]))

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ protocol
    h.append("<h2>3. Protocol and its limits</h2>")
    h.append("<ul><li><b>Failure series.</b> As defined in the terms box. A failure lasts about 10 s (7 frames at 1.5 s); the memory runs on these frames (decay 0.95 per frame, 1.5 s apart). Each cell has 137 clean-half and 154 failure-half frames; scenes are 22, so frames of a scene are strongly correlated. A failure that begins at the start of a drive (empty memory) and a long failure are not tested.</li><li><b>Metrics.</b> S at the champion's fixed threshold and assembly. Paired scene bootstrap (2000 draws over 22 scenes) for F1@1 differences, and for route-success differences (mean of the two seeds); <b>no interval exists for S</b>, so S differences below about 0.01 are not interpreted. The route head has two seeds that differ by 0.05 to 0.09.</li><li><b>Where the test frames are.</b> Untouched. But the settings of this loop were chosen with these validation scenes in view: hold decay 0.95 and EMA 0.5 (earlier loops), the gate design and its training length, 'recursion off' after E196, and the design of the map builder after the diagnosis E205. The four milder failure types (4.2) were not used for any choice. The numbers are validation numbers with design choices on them.</li><li><b>Failures are synthetic.</b> They are perturbed images and dropped sensors, not recorded drives. The builder's training uses the same kinds of perturbation as the test series (on training scenes); an unseen failure type is tested in section 4.7d.</li><li><b>Maps.</b> The SD map is simulated from the lane map (loop 7: lane count, shifted polylines, dropped roads); 'exact' means perfect registration, and the registration error is assumed independent of the sensor failure.</li><li><b>Latency</b> excludes the foundation network.</li></ul>")

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ results
    h.append("<h2>4. Experiments</h2>")
    h.append("<h3>4.1 E193 / E208: what a failure costs, and the matched clean reference</h3>")
    h.append(f"<p><b>Hypothesis.</b> Both chains read the same 42 channels, so the champion loses as much as the control. <b>Result.</b> Yes in S: {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'champ_single'))} (champion) against {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'ctrl_single'))} (control). In F1@1 the champion is lower at night and in blur ({b('dark','champ_single - ctrl_single')}, {b('blur','champ_single - ctrl_single')}). The loss is mainly lanes not found ({f3(S('dark','champ_single',m='lanes_found'),2)} / {f3(S('blur','champ_single',m='lanes_found'),2)} / {f3(S('cam_front3_off','champ_single',m='lanes_found'),2)} against 0.71 on the clean half), but precision falls too (P@1 {f3(S('dark','champ_single',m='P@1.0'),2)} / {f3(S('blur','champ_single',m='P@1.0'),2)} / {f3(S('cam_front3_off','champ_single',m='P@1.0'),2)} against 0.91) and false length rises four-fold. <b>Matched reference (E208).</b> The clean half is a different part of each scene than the failure half; the same failure-half frames without any perturbation score S {f3(ref.get('dark', {}).get('champ_single', {}).get('S', float('nan')))} (champion, single frame) and {f3(ref.get('dark', {}).get('champ_hold', {}).get('S', float('nan')))} with the hold, against {f3(cl)} on the clean half: the two references agree within 0.01 S, so the failure-half losses in this report are not an artefact of scene content. <b>Conclusion.</b> A sparse graph fails mostly by absence and also by false lanes.</p>")
    for p in P3:
        h.append(f"<h3 style='font-size:9pt'>{esc(NAME[p])}</h3>" + g["tbl_main"](p, ["ctrl_single", "champ_single"], ref={"champ_single": "ctrl_single"}))
    h.append("<h3>4.2 E194: four milder failure types</h3>")
    h.append("<p><b>Hypothesis.</b> Milder failures lose less. <b>Result.</b> Yes: fog, radar off and one front camera off cost 0.04 to 0.08 S, an extrinsics error 0.13. Reference of the hold columns: the recursion-on champion.</p>" + g["tbl_more"]() + f"<p><b>Conclusion.</b> The hold gains F1@1 on all four (extrinsics {b('extr3','champ_hold - champ_single')}) but S stays flat and J falls (0.25 to 0.12 for extrinsics, and 0.03 to 0.04 on the other three): the hold costs junction quality, not only under an extrinsics error. Night and blur are the hard cases.</p>")
    h.append("<h3>4.3 E195 / E196: memory on the input, and the builder's own recursion</h3>")
    h.append("<p><b>Hypothesis.</b> Most of the failure loss is lost input, so a memory in front of the builder repairs it; the builder's recursion should help too. <b>Design.</b> Spatial hold: per pixel the decayed (0.95), ego-motion-warped previous input replaces the current frame where its lane heat is higher. EMA 0.5 with a label-free heat monitor is the second variant (warp = grid_sample, both parameter-free and differentiable). <b>Result.</b> The hold wins back the S loss to the shares given in the summary (a share is the gain divided by the loss of the single frame: night " + f"{share('dark','champ_hold')*100:.0f} %, blur {share('blur','champ_hold')*100:.0f} %, three cameras {share('cam_front3_off','champ_hold')*100:.0f} %). EMA + monitor hold is about the same as the hold (night {f3(S('dark','champ_ema'))}, blur {f3(S('blur','champ_ema'))}, cameras {f3(S('cam_front3_off','champ_ema'))}) with a better clean half (0.760); the monitor does not fire for lost cameras. Recursion off changes the failure half by at most 0.01 S (and F1@1 by {b('dark','norec_hold - champ_hold')} at night). <b>Conclusion.</b> The memory that works is the one on the input. The recursion gives nothing in S; on clean route success it is worth about 0.02 (0.857 with, 0.838 without), which is within the seed spread.</p>")
    for p in P3:
        h.append(f"<h3 style='font-size:9pt'>{esc(NAME[p])}</h3>" + g["tbl_main"](p, ["ctrl_hold", "champ_single", "champ_hold", "champ_ema", "norec_single", "norec_hold"], ref={"champ_hold": "champ_single", "champ_ema": "champ_single", "norec_hold": "champ_hold"}))
    h.append(figure("p17_summary.png", "(1) Failure-half S by failure type and system (the axis starts at 0.3); (2) route success under failure; (3) the map builder on synthetic loss of perception (E205); (4) the map builder trained on synthetic loss only, on the perturbed series (E204). Panels 3 and 4 refer to section 4.7.", 96))
    h.append("<h3>4.4 E199: a learned gated memory</h3>")
    h.append("<p><b>Hypothesis.</b> A learned gate beats the training-free hold because it can see when the current frame is bad and when the memory is old. <b>Architecture.</b> The memory y(t-1) is warped by the ego motion. A small conv net (11 input channels: heat and direction of frame and warped memory, the hold decision, validity, a feature-mismatch map and two feature-energy maps; 3 convolutions, 12.5 k parameters) outputs two gates, one for the 39 feature channels and one for heat / direction; y = g * memory + (1 - g) * frame. It starts as the hold (checked: it reproduces the hold to 0.5 to 0.7 % of the signal), the builder is frozen, the loss is the builder loss of frame t, three frames are unrolled with the memory detached before the first. Training: 1500 steps (12 minutes) on failure and clean frames of the scenes of the other fold. <b>Result.</b> A gain at night and in blur, none with lost cameras; the clean half rises to 0.760 (hold 0.752; EMA 0.760). <b>Conclusion.</b> The hold is nearly as good and free; the gate adds about 0.02 S at night and in blur and needs failure data. One training run per fold: the intervals cover the choice of scenes, not the training seed.</p>")
    for p in P3:
        h.append(f"<h3 style='font-size:9pt'>{esc(NAME[p])}</h3>" + g["tbl_main"](p, ["norec_single", "norec_hold", "gate", "ctrl_hold"], ref={"norec_hold": "norec_single", "gate": "norec_hold", "ctrl_hold": "norec_single"}))
    h.append(figure("p17_frames_dark.png", "Qualitative frames, night, failure half. Columns: the 10th / 35th / 65th / 90th percentile of the per-frame F1@1 difference gate minus single frame (chosen by the number). Rows: ground truth (green band), single frame, spatial hold, learned gate, control with hold. Illustration, not evidence: in two of the four columns the gate is below the hold.", 78))
    h.append(figure("p17_frames_blur.png", "The same for motion blur.", 78))
    h.append("<h3>4.5 E197 / E198: failure-trained input stage and failure-frame fine-tuning</h3>")
    h.append("<p><b>Hypothesis.</b> A robust input stage, or a builder fine-tuned on failure frames, repairs what the hold cannot. <b>Design.</b> E197: the dense head of loop 5 trained on perturbed training frames replaces the dense head. E198: the builder fine-tuned 1.5 u on half clean training frames, half failure-half frames of the other fold's scenes (about 77 frames per type and fold, out-of-fold). <b>Result.</b> E197 improves single frames at night (F1@1 " + f"{b('dark','ftall_single - champ_single')}) and is no better with the hold. E198: single-frame F1@1 {b('dark','ft_single - champ_single')} at night, {b('blur','ft_single - champ_single')} in blur; with the hold and three cameras off it is worse ({b('cam_front3_off','ft_hold - champ_hold')}). <b>Conclusion.</b> With about 80 frames per type the builder does not improve; with 842 perturbed rows per type it does (section 4.7). The sample size, not the principle, decided.</p>")
    for p in P3:
        h.append(f"<h3 style='font-size:9pt'>{esc(NAME[p])}</h3>" + g["tbl_main"](p, ["champ_single", "ftall_single", "ft_single", "champ_hold", "ftall_hold", "ft_hold"], ref={"ftall_single": "champ_single", "ft_single": "champ_single", "ftall_hold": "champ_hold", "ft_hold": "champ_hold"}))
    h.append("<h3>4.6 E200 / E201: map assignment under failure</h3>")
    h.append(f"<p><b>Hypothesis.</b> The route head along the edges needs the lane the route runs on, so route success falls with the lanes found. <b>Design.</b> The route head (two seeds, trained on clean tokens) on the graphs of the series, SD map without error. <b>Result.</b> Route success falls much further than the graph score and is brought back only in part by the hold and the gate. Single-frame rows are recursion on, hold and gate rows recursion off (an overwritten file); the single frame at night has a graph in 111 of the 137 frames with a route (the others are empty and dropped, which flatters it). Scene-bootstrap intervals of the mean of both seeds: hold against single frame {rb('dark: hold - single frame (champion; hold = recursion off)')} (night), {rb('blur: hold - single frame (champion; hold = recursion off)')} (blur), {rb('cam_front3_off: hold - single frame (champion; hold = recursion off)')} (three cameras); gate against hold {rb('dark: gate - hold')} / {rb('blur: gate - hold')} / {rb('cam_front3_off: gate - hold')}. <b>Conclusion.</b> Route success follows lanes found; it is associated with them, an oracle graph into the route head was not tried.</p>" + g["tbl_route"]())
    h.append("<h3>4.7 E204 - E207, E209, E210: does the SD map fill what perception lost?</h3>")
    h.append("<p><b>Hypothesis.</b> A builder that sees the SD map as five extra input channels can add lanes where the perception is blind. <b>Design.</b> The first stem convolution is widened (new weights zero, so the network starts as the champion). <b>E204</b>: training shows the map with pose error 0 to 3 m, a wrong map (10 %) or none (10 %), and removes the perception in 40 % of the frames on purpose (global attenuation of heat, direction and features, or rectangles with no evidence); the perturbed series is never seen. Result on the perturbed series:</p>" + g["tbl_map"]())
    h.append(f"<p><b>E205 diagnosis.</b> On validation frames with the same synthetic loss as in training the picture is different: the champion falls to S 0.463 (lanes found 0.16), the map builder reaches 0.678 without a map (0.56 found), 0.700 with an exact map (0.69), 0.672 with a 2 m error and 0.646 with a wrong map. So the degraded-input training gave most of the gain on the synthetic frames (+0.215), the exact map +0.022, and a wrong map is worse than none by 0.032. On the perturbed series E204 gains {S('dark','map_single_m0')-S('dark','norec_single'):+.3f} / {S('blur','map_single_m0')-S('blur','norec_single'):+.3f} / {S('cam_front3_off','map_single_m0')-S('cam_front3_off','norec_single'):+.3f} S on single frames (F1@1 {b('dark','map_single_m0 - norec_single')} at night): the synthetic loss is not the real one.</p>")
    if g["has_e139"]:
        h.append(g["e139_html"]())
    # ---- unseen failure types, map-only, consumer
    h.append(g["extra_html"]())
    h.append("<h3>4.8 E202: joint end-to-end fine-tune</h3>")
    h.append("<p><b>Hypothesis.</b> The route loss through the builder improves the route success. <b>Design.</b> Builder (trainable, beam 192 / 96, recursion off) -> beam assembly (discrete: it decides which nodes and edges exist) -> the builder's node tokens gathered at the surviving vertices and interpolated along the edges by a fixed sparse matrix -> route head (trainable, cross-attention to the SD raster) -> route label per point. Arms: a = route loss + builder loss (weight 1), b = route loss weighted 20 times higher, c = control as b with the tokens detached, so the route loss does not reach the builder (the builder still trains on its own loss, weight 0.05, and the route head keeps training). 600 iterations, batch 4, from the champion (beam 192 / 96) and the route head of seed 0. <b>Result.</b> The route loss on training frames is only 0.03 (the head already fits them), so the gradient into the builder is small (the total builder gradient norm is 6.7 in arm a, dominated by the builder loss; 0.55 in b against 0.38 in c). Route success (seed 0, exact map; about 137 frames per half, standard error about 0.03 to 0.04; one value per cell):</p>")
    rows = []
    for nm, tag in (("base: champion + route head seed 0", "base"), ("a: joint, builder loss weight 1", "a"), ("b: joint, route loss x20", "b"), ("c: control, route loss does not reach the builder", "c")):
        r = [esc(nm)]
        for series in ("dark_single", "dark_maxdecay_0.95", "blur_maxdecay_0.95"):
            c = MJ["route"].get(f"e136_route/{series}_{tag}", {})
            for ph in ("cl", "fail"):
                xs = [v["route_success"] for k, v in c.items() if k.endswith(f"_{ph}")]
                r.append(f3(xs[0]) if xs else "-")
        rows.append(r)
    h.append(table(["arm", "night single: clean", "failure", "night hold: clean", "failure", "blur hold: clean", "failure"], rows, nums=set(range(1, 7))))
    h.append("<p>Graph quality: clean-half S 0.746 to 0.750 (single frames), failure-half S within 0.007 of the base in all arms. <b>Conclusion.</b> The gradient path works. The experiment cannot show an effect: the arms differ among themselves by up to 0.09 in route success (clean half 0.80 to 0.89), which is noise at this sample size; the gain of arm c over the base is as large as that of the joint arms, so it is the extra route-head training. Not tested: a planner loss, more than one seed, a route head not fitted to the training frames, the gate trained with the route loss.</p>")
    h.append("<h3>4.9 E203: time per frame</h3>")
    h.append("<p>Median over 60 validation frames, batch 1, idle RTX 3060, cached foundation features (the foundation network and the dense head are not timed; the GaussianCaR paper reports 12 FPS on an RTX 4090), CUDA graphs for the builder and the route head, CPU assembly. The memory stage is the warp of the 42 channels and the three convolutions of the gate. The total is the median of the per-frame sums, so the columns do not add exactly; the 90th percentile of the total is 42 to 56 ms. The rasterisation of the SD map for the map-conditioned builder is not timed.</p>" + g["tbl_latency"]())

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ understanding
    h.append("<h2>5. Where it falls short, and why</h2>")
    h.append("<ul><li><b>Lanes found and precision.</b> With the champion and the memory the failure half finds 0.43 to 0.50 of the lanes (0.71 to 0.74 on the clean half) with precision 0.71 to 0.79 (0.91 clean); with the map builder trained on perturbed rows 0.63 to 0.67. The part that is not found is not in the features of the frames that are perturbed, as far as the champion's weights are concerned; training changes what the same features give (section 4.7).</li><li><b>Route success follows lanes found</b> (associated, not shown causal): 0.4 to 0.6 against 0.85 for the champion with memory.</li><li><b>Junctions.</b> J falls to 0.15 to 0.19 (0.25 clean) and the hold lowers it a little on the clean half too (reason not isolated).</li><li><b>Extrinsics.</b> The hold raises F1@1 (+0.06) but not S; J falls. The reason is not isolated; the literature cards (agent B) report no evaluation of extrinsic-error robustness on the lane output of a camera-radar network.</li><li><b>Synthetic loss is not real failure.</b> Training on synthetic loss of perception gave +0.2 S on synthetic frames and +0.008 to +0.027 on the perturbed series; perturbed training rows transferred.</li><li><b>The map is a new dependency.</b> The benefit needs a registration error below about 1 m, and a wrong map is worse than no map; the chain has no trust signal for the map, and the label-free agreement of loop 8 fails at night (AUROC 0.62).</li></ul>")
    h.append("<h2>6. Best methods so far, and what is open</h2>")
    h.append(f"<div class='box'><p><b>Best chain without a map.</b> Champion builder + frozen foundation and dense head + <b>learned gated memory on the input</b> (12.5 k parameters, starts as the hold) + route head along the edges + consumer; recursion off. Failure-half S {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'gate'))} against {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'norec_single'))} without memory ({f3(S('dark','gate','cl'))} on the clean half); route success about {rs('e135_route/dark_gate'):.2f} / {rs('e135_route/blur_gate'):.2f} / {rs('e135_route/cam_front3_off_gate'):.2f}. Cost: 12 minutes of training per fold on failure frames; the hold alone needs no training and gives {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'norec_hold'))}.</p><p><b>Best chain if the map is good to about 1 m.</b> The map-conditioned builder trained on perturbed rows behind the gate: S {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'gateB_m0'))} with an exact map, {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'gateB_m1'))} with 1 m, {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'gateB_m2'))} with 2 m, {trio(lambda p: S(p, 'gateB_mnone'))} without a map; route success {rs('e140_route/dark_gateB_B'):.2f} / {rs('e140_route/blur_gateB_B'):.2f} / {rs('e140_route/cam_front3_off_gateB_B'):.2f} with an exact map. Exact maps are an upper bound (section 4.7).</p>" + figure("p17_best.png", "The chains for the three main failure types. Left: failure-half S (axis from 0.38). Right: route success (champion single frame is recursion on, the others recursion off; two seeds averaged).", 96) + "<p><b>Open, with the cheapest first.</b> (1) A trust signal for the SD map (a dustbin or matchability output; days of work; without it the map chain must not be used). (2) More perturbed scenes for the builder and the gate (the training saw 842 rows per type, the gate 11 scenes per fold); tested here only with the same failure types except section 4.7d. (3) Recorded failures instead of perturbed features, and a registered navigation map. (4) A planner loss and a planner-level evaluation under failure beyond section 4.7e. (5) A TEST run of the route head along the edges, the learned gate and the map builder: this needs your decision; TEST has been used once, in loop 16. A reasonable go criterion: failure-half S within 0.08 of the clean half and route success above 0.7 without a map, or with a map only if the trust signal exists.</p></div>")
    h.append("<h2>7. Limits of the experiments, in short</h2>")
    h.append("<ul><li>Validation only; settings chosen with these scenes in view.</li><li>22 scenes, 154 failure frames per cell, strongly correlated; no interval for S.</li><li>Route head: two seeds, spread 0.05 to 0.09.</li><li>The gate and the fine-tune see 11 scenes per fold; one training run each.</li><li>Failures are synthetic, half a scene long, with the same types in training and test except section 4.7d.</li><li>The SD map is derived from the lane map; registration error independent of the failure.</li><li>Latency without the foundation network.</li><li>Literature statements come from page digests.</li></ul>")
    h.append("<h2>Appendix: experiments and files</h2>")
    h.append(table(["id", "internal run", "what", "script", "result file"],
                   [["E193 / E194", "E130 / E130b", "champion on the failure series, seven types", "p17_series.py, p17_eval.py", "e17/e130_champion, e130b_more"],
                    ["E195", "E131", "spatial hold, EMA + monitor hold", "p17_eval.py", "e17/e131_hold"],
                    ["E196", "E132", "builder recursion off", "p17_eval.py --prev 0", "e17/e132_norec"],
                    ["E197 / E198", "E133a / b", "failure-trained dense head; failure-frame fine-tune, two folds", "p17_dense.py, p17_ft.py, p17_merge.py", "e17/e133a_ftall, e133b_k0 / k1"],
                    ["E199", "E134", "learned gated memory, two folds", "p17_gate.py", "e17/e134_gate"],
                    ["E200 / E201", "E135 / E135b", "route success on the series; hold and gate without recursion", "p17_route.py", "e17/e135_route"],
                    ["E202", "E136", "joint end-to-end fine-tune, three arms", "p17_joint.py", "e17/e136_route, e136_graph_*"],
                    ["E203", "E137", "time per frame", "p17_latency.py", "e17/latency"],
                    ["E204 / E205", "E138 / E138b", "map-conditioned builder, synthetic loss; diagnosis", "p17_mapnet.py, p17_mapft.py, p17_mapdiag.py", "e17/e138_map, e138b_mapdiag.json"],
                    ["E206", "E139", "map builder B and control C on perturbed training rows", "p17_dense_train.py, p17_mapft.py", "e17/e139_b, e139_c, e139_route"],
                    ["E207", "E140", "gate behind the map builder; route success", "p17_gate.py --mapnet", "e17/e140_gateB, e140_route"],
                    ["E208", "E141", "matched clean reference", "p17_series.py --clean", "e17/e141_clean, e141_cleanB"],
                    ["E209", "E142", "unseen failure types", "p17_mapft.py --fail_perts", "e17/e142"],
                    ["E210", "E143", "map-only baseline", "p17_maponly.py", "e17/e143_maponly.json"],
                    ["E212", "E144", "planner consumer under failure", "p17_consumer.py", "e17/e144_champ, e144_map"],
                    ["numbers", "", "every number of this report", "p17_master.py, p17_extra.py", "e17/results_master.json, results_extra.json"]]))
